{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And this staff.</b> He said <b>this</b> in accordance with what they said in the Zohar (Zohar vol. 2, 28a) that there were two staffs; by saying ״this״ he excludes the other." "And his saying <b>you shall take in your hand</b> means constantly, and the reason is as a sign that in his hand is a staff of might, of splendor, and so it is a statute for authorities appointed over the affairs of the kingdom. And this is your sign, as it is written (Exodus 4:20): ״וַיָּשָׁב אַרְצָה מִצְרַיִם וַיִּקַּח וְגוֹ׳ מַטֵּה הָאֱלֹהִים בְּיָדוֹ״ — see that he immediately donned the weapon that HaShem had given him, for if the intent of the taking were merely that he took it to bring it along with him, it would not have been necessary to say ״in his hand.״" "And his saying <b>with which you shall perform the signs</b>: perhaps he alluded to the second staff, that of Aharon, with which he performed the signs — when it turned into a serpent (Exodus 7:10) — as they say there in the Zohar that this is called the staff of Aharon, for He commanded also concerning it that it be ready in Aharon's hand. Further, it alludes in the manner of their statement of blessed memory (Targum Yonatan, Exodus 2:21) that the staff was a sappirinon, and no person could move it except Mosheh; accordingly, his taking it would indicate that it is with it that he will perform the signs, and this is your sign that no stranger other than Mosheh may approach the staff."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:24:31.250721297Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}